WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.714 --> 00:00:10.821
Imagine being separated from your family and friends for many years, even though they live just a few

2
00:00:10.901 --> 00:00:14.043
hundred meters away on the other side of a concrete wall.

3
00:00:14.824 --> 00:00:20.389
If you attempt to bypass the wall to see them, you will be severely punished or maybe killed

4
00:00:20.469 --> 00:00:21.450
by armed guards.

5
00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:28.475
This scenario may sound implausible, but this is the true story of the people affected by the Berlin

6
00:00:28.496 --> 00:00:32.298
Wall, a barrier that once divided East and West Berlin.

7
00:00:33.279 --> 00:00:40.083
At the End of World War II After World War II, Germany was split into four zones, which

8
00:00:40.123 --> 00:00:42.725
were each occupied by a different foreign army.

9
00:00:43.586 --> 00:00:51.391
The capitalist French, British, and Americans managed the western parts of the country, while the communist Soviets controlled

10
00:00:51.411 --> 00:00:52.452
the eastern section.

11
00:00:53.192 --> 00:00:58.988
Capitalists believe that individuals should have the right to make their own money in a market economy and

12
00:00:59.088 --> 00:01:00.089
keep what they earn.

13
00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:07.675
Communists, on the other hand, believe that wealth should be spread equally across a society, so the rich

14
00:01:07.775 --> 00:01:12.059
must give up some of their property to be redistributed to poorer families.

15
00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:19.525
The conflict between these two belief systems, and the nations that espoused them, became known as the Cold

16
00:01:19.565 --> 00:01:20.686
War (1947-1991).

17
00:01:20.706 --> 00:01:30.833
This struggle for dominance between the United States and the Soviet Union led to raised military and political

18
00:01:30.893 --> 00:01:32.294
tensions around the world.

19
00:01:33.134 --> 00:01:40.137
In Berlin, as in the rest of Germany, relations between communists and capitalists were particularly tense.

20
00:01:41.138 --> 00:01:48.141
Located deep inside East Germany, the capital had also been divided into two zones; West Berlin was in

21
00:01:48.181 --> 00:01:54.384
the hands of the allied British, French, and American governments, and the bigger East Berlin was controlled by

22
00:01:54.424 --> 00:01:55.084
the Soviets.

23
00:01:55.892 --> 00:02:03.814
Hostility between them grew during the 1950s, when millions of skilled workers left impoverished East Berlin to seek

24
00:02:03.874 --> 00:02:05.935
employment in the more prosperous West.

25
00:02:07.055 --> 00:02:14.037
At the same time, many West Berliners entered the communist zone to buy cheap groceries at stores subsidized

26
00:02:14.117 --> 00:02:15.377
by the Soviet government.

27
00:02:16.237 --> 00:02:22.459
The East was losing its labor force while shoppers were taking advantage of the communist area's lower prices.

28
00:02:23.399 --> 00:02:31.199
Facing total economic collapse, East German leader Walter Ulbricht proposed building a barrier to prevent people from crossing

29
00:02:31.299 --> 00:02:32.980
over from one side to the other.

30
00:02:33.941 --> 00:02:40.845
The Construction of the Berlin Wall Construction on the barrier began on August 13, 1961.

31
00:02:41.785 --> 00:02:45.187
Many people were suddenly separated from their families and friends.

32
00:02:46.087 --> 00:02:51.050
West Berliners who happened to be shopping in East Berlin that day were informed that they would not

33
00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:57.149
be able to return home without risking their lives; East Berlin workers were cut off from their jobs

34
00:02:57.209 --> 00:02:57.789
in the West.

35
00:02:58.509 --> 00:03:00.970
Berlin was now a physically divided city.

36
00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:05.231
At first the fence only consisted of barbed wire.

37
00:03:05.951 --> 00:03:11.153
It was 155 kilometers long and sat just inside Soviet territory.

38
00:03:12.013 --> 00:03:18.275
To deter people from trying to cross, armed East German troops stood at regular intervals and were ordered

39
00:03:18.295 --> 00:03:21.816
to shoot anyone who attempted to enter or to leave East Berlin.

40
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.361
Then, in June 1962, construction on another fence was started.

41
00:03:28.202 --> 00:03:34.643
The second barrier ran parallel to the first, but left a wide-open space of 91 meters between them.

42
00:03:35.464 --> 00:03:40.965
This area was covered in gravel to make it easy for guards to spot footprints left by people

43
00:03:41.025 --> 00:03:44.046
trying to escape, and filled with landmines.

44
00:03:44.906 --> 00:03:50.708
Because so many people were killed trying to cross this alley into West Berlin, it became known as

45
00:03:50.788 --> 00:03:51.588
the death strip.

46
00:03:52.464 --> 00:03:58.507
The final two phases of the wall's construction began in 1965 and 1975.

47
00:03:59.348 --> 00:04:04.290
The East German troops replaced the barbed wire fence with a solid concrete wall.

48
00:04:05.191 --> 00:04:13.755
Then they fortified it; in the end it had 45,000 different sections, each 3.6 meters high and 1.2

49
00:04:13.815 --> 00:04:14.616
meters wide.

50
00:04:15.436 --> 00:04:22.060
There were also 116 watchtowers, 20 bunkers, and only 8 gates, each with armed guards.

51
00:04:23.144 --> 00:04:30.689
Famous Escape Attempts Despite such great obstacles, more than five thousand people did manage to flee from East

52
00:04:30.709 --> 00:04:32.050
Berlin to West Berlin.

53
00:04:32.871 --> 00:04:39.055
The first escapees were able to jump over the barbed wire fence through an apartment window or from

54
00:04:39.095 --> 00:04:40.676
the roof of a nearby building.

55
00:04:41.737 --> 00:04:50.603
In 1964 West Berliners were able to help 57 people escape through a 145-meter-long tunnel dug under the

56
00:04:50.643 --> 00:04:51.384
wire fence.

57
00:04:52.368 --> 00:04:58.752
After the completion of the concrete wall, however, defectors had to become more creative in their escape plans.

58
00:04:59.672 --> 00:05:05.456
Some were able to escape by sliding along a cable suspended over the fence between two buildings.

59
00:05:06.596 --> 00:05:12.460
One man was able to drive a low sports car under the barricade into freedom at Checkpoint Charlie,

60
00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:14.281
the main crossing point.

61
00:05:15.341 --> 00:05:21.805
Perhaps the most famous story of a successful escape is that of Thomas Kruger, who piloted a small

62
00:05:21.905 --> 00:05:26.628
aircraft, stolen from an East German military training camp, into West Berlin.

63
00:05:27.409 --> 00:05:33.092
After landing safely he dismantled the plane and sent it in pieces to the East Germans with written

64
00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:35.394
messages such as "Wish you were here."

65
00:05:36.355 --> 00:05:41.958
Unfortunately, not everyone who tried to escape East Germany made it across the border alive.

66
00:05:42.779 --> 00:05:46.541
As many as 239 people were killed trying to cross.

67
00:05:47.402 --> 00:05:53.986
The most famous case happened in 1962, when a young man named Peter Fletcher was shot as he

68
00:05:54.026 --> 00:05:55.427
was scaling the outer wall.

69
00:05:56.247 --> 00:06:02.050
He fell into the death strip, and even though he was still alive, neither onlookers nor the media

70
00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:06.553
could offer him assistance, as East German guards would certainly have shot them.

71
00:06:07.353 --> 00:06:10.095
Fletcher's death was captured on film by the media.

72
00:06:10.995 --> 00:06:17.058
As the images of this horrible event circulated around the world, they became a symbol of communist tyranny.

73
00:06:18.059 --> 00:06:24.407
The Fall of the Berlin Wall Almost forty years after the official separation of East and West Berlin,

74
00:06:24.867 --> 00:06:28.650
the prospect of a unified Germany seemed as out of reach as ever.

75
00:06:29.451 --> 00:06:32.753
But several unexpected events occurred in 1989.

76
00:06:33.533 --> 00:06:41.179
In August of that year, communist Hungary and capitalist Austria opened their borders to each other, allowing thirteen

77
00:06:41.279 --> 00:06:44.161
thousand East Germans to flee via this new route.

78
00:06:44.961 --> 00:06:51.478
Huge demonstrations began to take place all over East Germany, which caused the leader of East Germany, Erich

79
00:06:51.558 --> 00:06:52.699
Honecker, to resign.

80
00:06:53.560 --> 00:07:00.045
The new East German leader, Egon Krenz, had decided to draft a policy that would allow East Berliners

81
00:07:00.085 --> 00:07:02.808
to apply for visas to travel to West Berlin.

82
00:07:03.808 --> 00:07:10.114
On November 9 the Minister of Propaganda, Gunter Schabowski, was scheduled to meet with the press.

83
00:07:11.315 --> 00:07:16.619
He was informed of the proposed changes, but was still unaware that the policy had not yet been

84
00:07:16.659 --> 00:07:22.860
finalized. He announced on the air that all East Berliners would be allowed to cross the border with

85
00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:23.841
proper permission.

86
00:07:24.622 --> 00:07:29.886
When asked how soon this policy would take effect, he responded that it was effective immediately.

87
00:07:30.727 --> 00:07:36.632
Tens of thousands of East Berliners who had listened to the minister's statement on television headed to the

88
00:07:36.672 --> 00:07:39.635
checkpoints, demanding that they be allowed to cross.

89
00:07:40.656 --> 00:07:44.980
The guards had no idea how to handle such crowds and could not control them.

90
00:07:45.788 --> 00:07:51.991
As thousands of East Berliners stampeded across the barrier, they were met by anxious families and old friends

91
00:07:52.091 --> 00:07:52.952
living in the West.

92
00:07:53.772 --> 00:07:59.235
Soon excited Germans were destroying the wall with sledgehammers and even their bare hands.

93
00:08:00.435 --> 00:08:06.198
The symbol of a divided country was being destroyed, and citizens rejoiced at the end of the painful

94
00:08:06.258 --> 00:08:13.142
era. Today little of the Berlin Wall remains standing, but a few sections have been left to remind

95
00:08:13.182 --> 00:08:15.603
Germans of this sad episode in their history.

96
00:08:16.624 --> 00:08:23.509
Germany has since reunited under a single democratic leadership, but the effects of the separation are long lasting.

97
00:08:24.449 --> 00:08:29.713
Many talk about the many differences in culture that emerged between the East and West during their time

98
00:08:29.793 --> 00:08:36.437
apart. But most are just happy that they have been reunited with their families and friends, and together

99
00:08:36.837 --> 00:08:38.999
they look toward a future as one nation.
